A write reached through a helper repaints the canvas once, not twice.

passCanvasReactivity appends a redraw to every handler that writes a var the
draw function reads, and gatherBlockMutations credits a call to its callee's
Writes -- so `@click { bump() }` already redraws for whatever `bump` writes.
Injecting into the owner's funcs as well, which is what an effect's `@mount`
and a timer's `@tick` need, gave `bump` one of its own: two full rasterisations
per click, the first against half-applied state.

`Writes` is what tells the two apart. The checker fills it in, so a body that
did not exist then -- an effect's mount, a tick -- has none, and those are
exactly the ones nothing credits to a caller. A lambda is asked separately and
always, because what runs one is a host scheduler rather than code the pass can
read.

The canvas here is drawn from a package-level var, which is the second half of
the same fix: the pass counted the owner's vars alone, so a canvas reading one
had an empty dep set and was collected as reactive by nothing at all.

deny out/go/fyne/model.go `(?m)^func \(m \*Model\) bump\(\) \{\n\tm\.frame = \(m\.frame \+ 1\)\n\tm\.__n1Ctx` -- the helper must not rasterise; its caller already does, and two per click means the first runs against state the handler has not finished writing

deny out/go/fyne/model.go `(?m)^\tstep := func\(\) \{\n\t\ttotal = \(total \+ 1\)\n\t\tm\.__n1Ctx` -- a lambda inside a declared func is credited to that func's Writes too, so the caller already redraws for it; the checker's effect walk descends into a lambda where gatherBlockMutations does not

-- app.sngl --
import d "sngl:ui/draw"
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        fyne
    }
}

var frame = 0

ui.window(title="t") {
    func bump() {
        // Through a lambda, because `Writes` covers one written inside a func
        // and the redraw must not land on both it and the caller.
        var step = func() {
            frame = frame + 1
        }
        step()
    }

    ui.vbox {
        d.canvas(width=40px, height=40px) {
            d.circle(cx=20.0, cy=20.0, r=float(frame), style={fill=#3366FF})
        }
        ui.button(text="go", @click { bump() })
    }
}
